Pods Stuck in Pending? Why Kubernetes Isn’t Scheduling Them

You deploy your workload, but the Pods stay in Pending. No crashes, no logs; just waiting.
This usually means the scheduler can’t find a suitable node.

Common causes:
- Insufficient CPU or memory on available nodes
- Node selectors or affinity rules not matching any node
- Taints without matching tolerations
- Persistent Volume claims not yet bound

How to troubleshoot:
- Run kubectl describe pod to check scheduling events
- Review node capacity and allocatable resources
- Validate affinity, tolerations, and selectors
- Check PVC and storage class status

Pending Pods aren’t broken, they’re waiting for a condition you haven’t met yet.
